// src/boot/uefi_status.pdx — R19-M2-005 (#791)
//
// EFI_STATUS decode + panic-on-fatal helpers for the paideia-native EFI
// stub. Every Boot Services wrapper landed in R19.M2 (uefi_alloc.pdx #787,
// uefi_locate.pdx #788, uefi_memmap.pdx #789, uefi_openproto.pdx #790)
// returns a raw EFI_STATUS in RAX. Callers in the discovery flow (GOP #792,
// ACPI RSDP walk #794, TCG2 #795) need a common way to (a) test whether a
// call succeeded, and (b) abort the pre-ExitBootServices path with a
// user-visible fingerprint if a call that MUST succeed did not. This file
// provides both, closing the R19.M2 wrapper layer.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// EFI_STATUS shape (UEFI 2.10 §II-4 + Appendix D)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// EFI_STATUS is `UINTN` (8 bytes on x86_64). The high bit (bit 63) is the
// error flag:
//
//   bit 63 = 0, low 63 = 0             → EFI_SUCCESS (0)
//   bit 63 = 0, low 63 != 0            → warning (non-fatal advisory —
//                                          firmware succeeded but wants to
//                                          flag a condition; e.g. UNKNOWN_GLYPH)
//   bit 63 = 1                         → error (fatal — the operation did
//                                          not complete; caller must treat
//                                          the result as invalid)
//
// This is why `efi_status_is_error` is a one-instruction bit-63 extract
// rather than a value-table lookup: the ABI reserves the flag bit
// specifically so callers can classify at O(1) without knowing the enumeration.
// The named constants below are for callers that want to *distinguish
// specific* error codes (retry EFI_NOT_READY, widen the buffer on
// EFI_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL, etc.) — the panic path only cares whether the
// high bit is set.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Constant selection
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// UEFI 2.10 Appendix D defines ~30 error codes + ~10 warning codes. The
// export set below picks the ~15 codes that the R19.M2/M3 wrappers can
// plausibly encounter or that callsites want to switch on:
//
//   - Every allocation call (AllocatePages/AllocatePool) can return
//     OUT_OF_RESOURCES.
//   - Every buffer-shaped call (GetMemoryMap, LocateHandleBuffer) can
//     return BUFFER_TOO_SMALL as the "please retry with a larger buffer"
//     signal.
//   - LocateProtocol / OpenProtocol return NOT_FOUND if the requested
//     GUID has no handle registered.
//   - I/O and read paths can return NOT_READY on transient conditions,
//     DEVICE_ERROR / MEDIA_CHANGED / NO_MEDIA on backing-store faults,
//     and TIMEOUT on hardware stalls (relevant to TCG2 measurement in #795).
//   - LOAD_ERROR / INVALID_PARAMETER / UNSUPPORTED / ACCESS_DENIED /
//     ABORTED / SECURITY_VIOLATION / VOLUME_CORRUPTED / WRITE_PROTECTED
//     round out the diagnostic surface for the M3 protocol layer and
//     the R19+ image-load path.
//
// The remaining Appendix D codes (CRC_ERROR, END_OF_MEDIA, END_OF_FILE,
// INVALID_LANGUAGE, COMPROMISED_DATA, HTTP_ERROR, protocol-specific
// warnings) are omitted at this milestone — no R19.M2/M3 wrapper produces
// them. Additions land under the milestone that first encounters them
// (mechanical: append `pub let EFI_<NAME> : u64 = 0x8000...;`).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// efi_panic_on_fatal contract
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Signature: efi_panic_on_fatal(status, message_ptr, message_len) -> ()
//
// Behaviour:
//   - If (status & (1 << 63)) == 0 (success or warning), return normally
//     — the caller continues execution.
//   - Otherwise, emit `message_ptr` via ConOut->OutputString (UEFI 2.10
//     §12.4.3) and enter an infinite cli/hlt spin. This is the R19.M2
//     analogue of `klog_panic` (src/kernel/core/klog/panic.pdx §M4-001):
//     minimum possible surface (no ring, no stack walk, no NMI broadcast —
//     none of those substrates exist pre-ExitBootServices), maximum
//     visibility (the CHAR16* banner lands on the firmware console the
//     operator is already staring at while OVMF boots).
//
// `message_ptr` MUST be a NUL-terminated CHAR16* (UEFI 2.10 §II-4 —
// OutputString walks until U+0000). Encode message strings via the
// uefi_strings.pdx pattern (`[u16; N]` with a trailing 0 element).
//
// `message_len` is accepted but not consumed at this milestone. It is
// reserved for a future bounded-output path — either a paideia-as-native
// caller that hands over a non-NUL-terminated CHAR16 buffer (in which case
// this wrapper would stage a length-prefixed copy into a scratch region
// with an appended U+0000 before dispatching to OutputString), or a
// M3+ diagnostic surface that wants to cap the emitted prefix on a
// long-error banner. Kept in the signature now so R19.M2 callsites do
// not need to rewrite their arg lists when that path lands (paideia-as
// enhancement `pa-r19-bounded-outputstring`, tracked in the R19 preflight
// retrospective). At the current milestone the parameter costs nothing —
// it is delivered in SysV RDX and simply not read by the wrapper body.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// OutputString dispatch — MS x64 marshalling
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// UEFI 2.10 §12.4.3:
//   EFI_STATUS (EFIAPI *) (
//     IN EFI_SIMPLE_TEXT_OUTPUT_PROTOCOL *This,   // arg1 (MS RCX)
//     IN CHAR16                          *String  // arg2 (MS RDX)
//   );
//
// Path (bit-63 already established as set, so we're in the error branch):
//
//   mov  rdx, rsi                         ; MS arg2 = message_ptr (SysV rsi)
//   mov  rax, [rip + _efi_system_table]   ; SystemTable*
//   mov  rcx, [rax + 64]                  ; MS arg1 = ConOut*  (EFI_ST_OFFSET_CON_OUT)
//   sub  rsp, 40                          ; 32-byte MS shadow + 8-byte align (8→0 mod 16)
//   call [rcx + 8]                        ; ConOut->OutputString (EFI_STO_OFFSET_OUTPUT_STRING)
//   add  rsp, 40                          ; symmetric unwind (defensive — the
//                                         ;   spin loop below never reads RSP,
//                                         ;   but keeping the frame paired
//                                         ;   preserves stack-unwinder friendliness
//                                         ;   for the future firmware-side
//                                         ;   diagnostics path)
//
// Non-fatal path returns via `ret` (RAX left unwritten — return type is `()`,
// no rax convention applies).
//
// Halt loop — matches klog_panic's Step 6 (src/kernel/core/klog/panic.pdx:54-57):
//
//   efi_panic_on_fatal_spin:
//     cli                       ; drop IF just in case (UEFI hands off with
//                               ;   IF=1 for firmware timer callbacks)
//     hlt                       ; wait for the next interrupt in low power
//     jmp efi_panic_on_fatal_spin
//
// The label prefix (`efi_panic_on_fatal_`) is deliberate: paideia-as
// reserves bare `loop` / `if` / etc. as language keywords (reference
// note: "paideia-as reserved labels"), so labels used from asm blocks
// must be namespaced by the enclosing function name.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Register discipline
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// SysV non-volatiles (RBX, RBP, R12-R15, RSP): both helpers touch none of
// them. `efi_status_is_error` uses only RAX; `efi_panic_on_fatal` uses
// RAX/RCX/RDX (all SysV+MS volatile) on the fatal path and returns
// through them unmodified on the success path. Therefore @no_frame is
// sound for both and neither helper needs a save/restore preamble.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Provisional `_efi_system_table` shadow
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// paideia-as v0.21 `build --target uefi-x86_64` compiles one .pdx
// translation unit at a time (tools/build-uefi-stub.sh preamble).
// Following the discipline in uefi_alloc.pdx / uefi_locate.pdx /
// uefi_memmap.pdx / uefi_openproto.pdx we redeclare `_efi_system_table`
// locally so this file remains a well-formed standalone .pdx module.
// When R19.M2 grows the stub into a multi-file build (paideia-as
// enhancement `pa-r19-multi-file-link`, tracked in the R19 preflight
// retrospective), the duplicate resolves to the single .data global
// defined in `uefi_stub.pdx` under a no-op rename.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Encoder gap: intra-function Jcc/Jmp label fixups on the PE emit path
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// paideia-as v0.21 `--target uefi-x86_64` (PE emit, cmd_build/pe.rs)
// captures LabelFixup sites for intra-function Jcc/Jmp targets when
// encoding, but does not call `patch_label_fixups` after section
// layout the way the ELF emit path (cmd_build/elf.rs:128) does. The
// sibling emitter-pe crate correctly collects each fixup into
// `EmitResult.label_fixups` (paideia-as-emitter-pe/src/text_emitter.rs:59-125);
// only the top-level PE build glue skips the patching step. Effect:
// `je efi_panic_on_fatal_ok`, `jmp efi_panic_on_fatal_spin`, and any
// other label reference inside a `--target uefi-x86_64` compile unit
// currently emit with a rel32 field of 0x00000000 (i.e. "jump to the
// next instruction") — visible in `objdump -d` as `0F 84 00 00 00 00`
// and `E9 00 00 00 00`.
//
// Consequence for this file: at source level the wrapper is correct
// against UEFI 2.10 §12.4.3 and the M4-001 klog_panic spin idiom;
// the compiled binary's runtime branch behaviour is wrong until the
// encoder patch lands. This mirrors the discipline established for
// direct-call relocations in uefi_locate.pdx ("direct-call
// relocations are not yet resolved for `--target uefi-x86_64`") —
// document at the source, keep the source authoritative, close the
// runtime gap when the encoder catches up.
//
// Tracked as paideia-as enhancement `pa-r19-pe-label-fixups`
// (mechanical: wire `super::fixup::patch_label_fixups(&emit_result.label_fixups, ...)`
// into `cmd_build/pe.rs::build_pe_object` after section layout, symmetric
// to `cmd_build/elf.rs`). The paideia-os side of the fix is a no-op
// rebuild — no source change here. Filed to the paideia-as repo under
// the cross-repo escalation rule when the R19.M2 OVMF integration
// (#802) first exercises a runtime label — the panic-path witness is
// the natural trigger. Until then, the R19.M2 discovery flow succeeds
// down the LocateProtocol / OpenProtocol happy path (straight-line
// wrappers, no labels — see uefi_locate.pdx / uefi_openproto.pdx),
// and this file's helpers are dormant on that path.

module UefiStatus = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // EFI_STATUS canonical values (UEFI 2.10 Appendix D)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Success + 15 most-used error codes. Warnings (bit 63 clear, low bits
  // non-zero) omitted at this milestone — no R19.M2/M3 wrapper acts on
  // them (they are informational; the successful-return semantic still
  // applies). Add on demand under the consuming milestone.
  //
  // All error values have bit 63 set → `efi_status_is_error` reports 1.

  pub let EFI_SUCCESS              : u64 = 0x0000000000000000
  pub let EFI_LOAD_ERROR           : u64 = 0x8000000000000001
  pub let EFI_INVALID_PARAMETER    : u64 = 0x8000000000000002
  pub let EFI_UNSUPPORTED          : u64 = 0x8000000000000003
  pub let EFI_BAD_BUFFER_SIZE      : u64 = 0x8000000000000004
  pub let EFI_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL     : u64 = 0x8000000000000005
  pub let EFI_NOT_READY            : u64 = 0x8000000000000006
  pub let EFI_DEVICE_ERROR         : u64 = 0x8000000000000007
  pub let EFI_WRITE_PROTECTED      : u64 = 0x8000000000000008
  pub let EFI_OUT_OF_RESOURCES     : u64 = 0x8000000000000009
  pub let EFI_VOLUME_CORRUPTED     : u64 = 0x800000000000000A
  pub let EFI_NO_MEDIA             : u64 = 0x800000000000000C
  pub let EFI_MEDIA_CHANGED        : u64 = 0x800000000000000D
  pub let EFI_NOT_FOUND            : u64 = 0x800000000000000E
  pub let EFI_ACCESS_DENIED        : u64 = 0x800000000000000F
  pub let EFI_TIMEOUT              : u64 = 0x8000000000000012
  pub let EFI_ABORTED              : u64 = 0x8000000000000015
  pub let EFI_SECURITY_VIOLATION   : u64 = 0x800000000000001A

  // Bit-63 mask — the "error" flag in EFI_STATUS. Exported so callsites
  // that prefer an explicit bitwise test over the wrapper helper can
  // share the constant, keeping the interpretation single-sourced.
  pub let EFI_ERROR_MASK           : u64 = 0x8000000000000000

  // ==========================================================================
  // Standalone-compile shadow of the entry-point latch (see file preamble).
  // Overridden by the definition in uefi_stub.pdx when the M2 multi-file
  // build lands.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let mut _efi_system_table    : u64 = 0

  // ==========================================================================
  // efi_status_is_error — bit-63 classifier
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Returns 1 if `status` has bit 63 set (error), else 0.
  //
  // Emitted body (unsafe, @no_frame):
  //   mov  rax, rdi     ; rax = status
  //   shr  rax, 63      ; extract bit 63 into bit 0 (rax now 0 or 1)
  //   ret
  //
  // The `shr r64, imm` instruction sets ZF based on the result, so callers
  // that want to branch immediately can chain a `jz`/`jnz` off the encoder-
  // generated flag state without a separate `cmp` — however most callers
  // will use the u64 return value directly (`if (efi_status_is_error(s))`),
  // matching the paideia-side ergonomics of `!= 0` guards elsewhere.

  pub let efi_status_is_error : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (status: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {},
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "R19-M2-005 (#791). Bit-63 extract on EFI_STATUS per UEFI 2.10 §II-4 (EFI_STATUS is UINTN; high bit set => error, per Appendix D). Two-instruction body: `mov rax, rdi` copies the SysV arg-0 status into the return register, `shr rax, 63` shifts bit 63 into bit 0 so RAX is exactly 0 (success or warning) or 1 (error). Pure computational leaf — no memory access, no effects, no capabilities. Non-volatiles untouched; only RAX (SysV+MS volatile) written. @no_frame sound.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        shr rax, 63;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // efi_panic_on_fatal — conditional abort with ConOut banner
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // If `status` indicates error (bit 63 set), emit the CHAR16* message via
  // ConOut->OutputString and enter an infinite cli/hlt spin. Otherwise,
  // return normally.
  //
  // `message_len` is accepted for signature stability against a future
  // bounded-output path; currently unread by the wrapper body (see file
  // preamble under "efi_panic_on_fatal contract").
  //
  // Emitted body (unsafe, @no_frame):
  //
  //   ; -- classify --
  //   mov  rax, rdi                             ; rax = status
  //   shr  rax, 63                              ; rax = error bit (0 or 1)
  //   cmp  rax, 0
  //   je   efi_panic_on_fatal_ok                ; success/warning → return
  //
  //   ; -- fatal path: OutputString(ConOut, message) --
  //   mov  rdx, rsi                             ; MS arg2 = CHAR16*  (SysV rsi)
  //   mov  rax, [rip + _efi_system_table]       ; SystemTable*
  //   mov  rcx, [rax + 64]                      ; MS arg1 = ConOut*  (EFI_ST_OFFSET_CON_OUT)
  //   sub  rsp, 40                              ; 32-byte MS shadow + 8-byte align
  //   call [rcx + 8]                            ; ConOut->OutputString (EFI_STO_OFFSET_OUTPUT_STRING)
  //   add  rsp, 40                              ; symmetric unwind
  //
  //   ; -- infinite halt --
  //   efi_panic_on_fatal_spin:
  //   cli
  //   hlt
  //   jmp  efi_panic_on_fatal_spin
  //
  //   ; -- non-fatal return --
  //   efi_panic_on_fatal_ok:
  //   ret

  pub let efi_panic_on_fatal : (u64, u64, u64) -> () !{sysreg, mem} @{} =
    fn (status: u64, message_ptr: u64, message_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { sysreg, mem },
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "R19-M2-005 (#791). Conditional abort helper: if the caller-supplied EFI_STATUS has bit 63 set (error, per UEFI 2.10 Appendix D), emit the CHAR16* banner at message_ptr via ConOut->OutputString (UEFI 2.10 §12.4.3) and enter an infinite cli/hlt spin; otherwise return normally. Bit-63 classification uses the same two-instruction `mov rax, rdi; shr rax, 63` sequence as efi_status_is_error, followed by `cmp rax, 0; je efi_panic_on_fatal_ok` to gate the fatal path (paideia-as v0.21 encoder note: `test r64, r64` is not yet supported per src/kernel/core/fs/fd_cloexec.pdx §2.3, hence the explicit `cmp rax, 0`). Fatal path marshals to MS x64: SysV rsi (message_ptr) copied to MS rdx (arg2 String), SystemTable pointer loaded rip-relative, ConOut pointer fetched via [rax + 64] (EFI_ST_OFFSET_CON_OUT) into MS rcx (arg1 This), 32-byte MS x64 shadow area plus 8-byte alignment fix (RSP transitions 8→0 mod 16 as MS x64 §II-2.3.4 requires pre-CALL) reserved via `sub rsp, 40`, indirect call issued via `FF /2 [rcx + 8]` (EFI_STO_OFFSET_OUTPUT_STRING). The symmetric `add rsp, 40` runs even though the following spin never reads RSP, keeping the stack-frame shape well-defined for future firmware-side unwinders. Halt loop mirrors src/kernel/core/klog/panic.pdx Step 6 (cli/hlt/jmp self) — the pre-ExitBootServices equivalent of klog_panic, minimum surface (no ring/NMI/rbp-walk substrate exists yet) and maximum visibility (banner lands on the firmware console the operator is already watching). message_len (SysV rdx) is accepted for signature stability against a future bounded-output path (paideia-as enhancement `pa-r19-bounded-outputstring`) but currently unread; OutputString itself walks until CHAR16 U+0000 per UEFI 2.10 §II-4, so callers MUST NUL-terminate message_ptr — uefi_strings.pdx encoding pattern applies. Label prefix `efi_panic_on_fatal_` matches the reserved-word discipline (`loop`/`if` are paideia-as keywords). Non-volatiles (RBX/RBP/R12-R15) untouched — only volatile GPRs (RAX/RCX/RDX) clobbered so @no_frame is sound. Non-fatal path exits via `ret`; fatal path never returns (jmp self).",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        shr rax, 63;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je efi_panic_on_fatal_ok;

        mov rdx, rsi;
        mov rax, [rip + _efi_system_table];
        mov rcx, [rax + 64];
        sub rsp, 40;
        call [rcx + 8];
        add rsp, 40;

      efi_panic_on_fatal_spin:
        cli;
        hlt;
        jmp efi_panic_on_fatal_spin;

      efi_panic_on_fatal_ok:
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame
}
